WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.500
Variables and data types.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.196
In Python, a variable doesn't work the way you'd expect.

3
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:09.167
Getting it right explains a whole family of bugs.

4
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:11.633
Here's the key idea.

5
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.633
A variable is just a name tag, tied to an object living somewhere in memory.

6
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.518
The object carries the type.

7
00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:19.933
The name carries nothing.

8
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:22.933
So the same name can move to a different object.

9
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:27.567
Now watch what happens when we assign a list to a second name.

10
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:30.367
First, the name targets is tied to a list.

11
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.348
Then backup is tied to that very same list.

12
00:00:33.348 --> 00:00:34.533
Nothing was copied.

13
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:38.200
So a change made through either name is visible through both.

14
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.533
This split decides how safe sharing is.

15
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:44.737
Numbers, strings, and tuples are immutable.

16
00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:47.100
Lists, dicts, and sets are mutable.

17
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.455
Changing an immutable value builds a new object.

18
00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:52.300
Mutable ones change in place.

19
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.199
So every name pointing at a mutable object sees the change.

20
00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:58.333
That's the number one beginner bug.

21
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:00.733
Here it is in code.

22
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.467
We tie backup to the same list as targets.

23
00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:08.167
The function appends to its parameter, which is just one more tag on that list.

24
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:12.433
And printing backup shows Titan, even though we never touched it directly.

25
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:15.633
Here's how this bites a real agent.

26
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:19.167
The agent hands one config dictionary to every tool.

27
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:23.500
One tool lowers the limit to three, just for its own API call.

28
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.000
Suddenly the NEO tool fetches only three results, because they share one dict.

29
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.133
The mistake is trusting a shared mutable object.

30
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:37.867
The fix is a deep copy, or a config object that can't be changed at all.

31
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:39.838
Let's recap.

32
00:01:39.838 --> 00:01:43.567
A variable is a name tag, and the object carries the type.

33
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:47.600
Immutable objects get replaced, mutable ones change in place.

34
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.867
Assignment never copies anything.

35
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:52.595
So watch shared mutable arguments.

36
00:01:52.595 --> 00:01:53.667
Next up: lists.
